---

---
<section class="py-24 bg-[var(--bg-secondary)]">
  <div class="max-w-[var(--container-max)] mx-auto px-4 sm:px-6 lg:px-8">
    <!-- Section Header -->
    <div class="mb-16 max-w-3xl" data-aos="fade-up">
      <div
        class="inline-flex items-center gap-2 px-3 py-1 rounded-md bg-[var(--color-primary)]/10 text-[var(--color-primary)] text-xs font-semibold mb-4 tracking-wider uppercase"
      >
        <i class="bi bi-gem"></i> The core idea
      </div>
      <h2
        class="text-[var(--h2-size)] font-[var(--h2-weight)] text-[var(--text-main)] mb-4 tracking-tight leading-tight"
      >
        Why Xeno.<span class="text-[var(--color-primary)]">JS</span>
      </h2>
      <p
        class="text-[var(--body-size)] text-[var(--text-muted)] leading-relaxed"
      >
Xeno.JS makes the application architecture explicit. <br>

It gives TypeScript applications clear boundaries for domain logic,
use cases, dependencies, request context, and cross-cutting execution. <br>

Keep the HTTP framework, UI framework, and infrastructure you already use.
Xeno.JS sits underneath them and gives the application layer a structure
that remains visible as the codebase grows.
      </p>
    </div>

    <!-- List of 3 Features -->
    <div class="grid grid-cols-1 md:grid-cols-3 gap-x-12 gap-y-10">
      <!-- Feature 1 -->
      <div
        class="flex flex-col pb-8 border-b border-[var(--color-primary)]"
        data-aos="fade-up"
        data-aos-delay="100"
      >
        <div class="text-[var(--color-primary)] text-3xl mb-5">
          <i class="bi bi-box-seam"></i>
        </div>
        <h3
          class="text-[var(--h3-size)] font-[var(--h3-weight)] text-[var(--text-main)] mb-3 mt-0"
        >
          Zero-Magic Inversion of Control
        </h3>
        <p class="text-[var(--text-muted)] text-[var(--body-size)] m-0">
          No decorators or directory scanning. Dependencies are registered explicitly, making application composition visible and deterministic.
        </p>
      </div>

      <!-- Feature 2 -->
      <div
        class="flex flex-col pb-8 border-b border-[var(--color-primary)]"
        data-aos="fade-up"
        data-aos-delay="200"
      >
        <div class="text-[var(--color-primary)] text-3xl mb-5">
          <i class="bi bi-arrow-left-right"></i>
        </div>
        <h3
          class="text-[var(--h3-size)] font-[var(--h3-weight)] text-[var(--text-main)] mb-3 mt-0"
        >
          Native CQRS & Pipeline
        </h3>
        <p class="text-[var(--text-muted)] text-[var(--body-size)] m-0">
          Commands and Queries make application intent explicit. Pipelines keep validation, logging, caching, resilience, and other cross-cutting concerns outside business logic.
        </p>
      </div>

      <!-- Feature 3 -->
      <div
        class="flex flex-col pb-8 border-b border-[var(--color-primary)]"
        data-aos="fade-up"
        data-aos-delay="300"
      >
        <div class="text-[var(--color-primary)] text-3xl mb-5">
          <i class="bi bi-layers"></i>
        </div>
        <h3
          class="text-[var(--h3-size)] font-[var(--h3-weight)] text-[var(--text-main)] mb-3 mt-0"
        >
          One Application Model
        </h3>
        <p class="text-[var(--text-muted)] text-[var(--body-size)] m-0">
          Use the same architectural language across Node.js and Vue: domain primitives, application contracts, commands, queries, pipelines, and explicit context.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>
